        content='The updated research plan is well-structured and addresses the previous feedback effectively. I have a few minor suggestions to further refine the methodology:\n\n1. **Clarify the Wiener Filter for CMB Removal:** In your idea description, you mention using a Wiener-filtered estimate of the primary CMB to isolate the kSZ signal. Ensure that in Step 1 or 2, you explicitly define how this filter is constructed (e.g., using the known $C_\\ell^{CMB}$ and $C_\\ell^{noise}$ power spectra). Since you have the ground-truth CMB, you can also perform a "perfect" subtraction as a control to quantify the theoretical limit of your Wiener-filtered approach.\n\n2. **Refine the Velocity Reconstruction (Step 3):** Linear theory reconstruction of the velocity field from a density field typically requires a bias factor ($b$) to relate the halo distribution to the underlying matter density. Since your halos have a range of masses, consider using a mass-weighted density field or a simple linear bias model to improve the reconstruction fidelity. Explicitly state that you will test the sensitivity of the $\\tau$ recovery to this bias parameter.\n\n3. **Weighting Scheme Consistency:** In Step 2, you define the weight $w_{ij} = \\frac{1}{\\sigma_{noise}^2 + \\sigma_{CMB}^2(\\ell_{ij})}$. Note that the CMB variance is scale-dependent. Ensure that the implementation of this weight correctly maps the angular separation $r_{ij}$ to the corresponding multipole $\\ell \\approx \\pi / \\theta_{ij}$ to properly account for the CMB power spectrum\'s contribution to the noise at that specific scale.\n\n4. **Mass-Binning Bias:** In Step 4, you mention "equal-width bins in $\\log_{10}(M)$." Given the log-uniform distribution of your catalog, this is appropriate. However, ensure that the pairwise estimator calculation within these bins accounts for the fact that pairs are formed between halos of potentially different masses. You should clarify if you are binning by the *average* mass of 